age?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_12361.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>67th of the 108 Parshwanaths - Shri Lodrava Parshwanath.</p><p>Main Deity: A black-colored image of Sahastraphana Parshwanath, made of touchstone, seated in the lotus (padmasana) posture, 30 inches high and 24 inches wide. Above the image&#x27;s head is a canopy of a thousand serpent hoods, which is why it is also known as Sahastraphana Parshwanath.</p><p>Location: This temple is in the village of Lodrava, near Jaisalmer, Rajasthan.</p><p>History: It is said that in ancient times, Lodravpur was a large and magnificent city, the capital of the Rajputs. India&#x27;s ancient university was also located here, and the place enjoyed renown throughout the world. At one time this kingdom was under King Sagar, who had two sons, Shridhar and Rajdhar. They were awakened to the truth by a Jain acharya and embraced Jainism. They had an enormous and magnificent temple of Shri Chintamani Parshwanath built here, which is mentioned in the prashasti (dedicatory inscription) of the &quot;Satdal Padmayantra,&quot; composed by the scholar Shri Sahajkirti Ganivarya, still found on the right side of the sanctum door of this temple. It is recorded that the renovation work was subsequently begun by Seth Shri Khimsi and completed by his son Shri Punsi. In the course of time, the entire city was destroyed in a fierce war between the rulers Bhojdev and Jaisalji (uncle and nephew), during which this temple too was damaged. The victorious Jaisalji founded his new capital and named it Jaisalmer. Wealthy and honored merchants there built temples within the Jaisalmer fort. At that time the image of Shri Chintamani Parshwanath was taken from here to Jaisalmer and reconsecrated in the newly built temple, where it still resides today.</p><p>The generous and devout merchant Seth Shri Dhiru Shah began the renovation of this ancient temple, and then set out with a pilgrimage group (sangh) for Shatrunjaya. By the time he returned from the pilgrimage, the renovation had already been completed. He then began searching for a beautiful, extraordinary, and truly unique image. By divine providence, two devoted craftsmen from Patan were on their way to Multan carrying two extraordinary and exquisite images of Sahastraphana Parshwanath made of touchstone — the finest works of their lifetime — when they stopped here to rest. That night, they had a dream through divine power telling them to give these images to Seth Dhirushah here. The generous Dhirushah, too, was inspired in a dream to accept these images. The next day, the two men set out searching for each other, and upon meeting, the seth acquired both images by paying their weight in gold, without haggling over price. For this reason, this image is also called &quot;Amulya Parshwanath&quot; (the priceless Parshwanath). The craftsmen were overjoyed. The wooden cart in which the craftsmen had brought the images is still preserved here today. This is the oldest and principal pilgrimage site among the panchtirthi (five sacred sites) of Jaisalmer. The craftsmanship here is of a most distinctive kind. Craftsmen of western Rajasthan enhanced the glory of Rajasthan by presenting varied styles of craftsmanship at every site. The ancient kalpavriksha (wish-fulfilling tree) can be seen only here. In Vikram Samvat 1675, these images were installed under the guidance of Acharya Jinrajguruji, and the installed images remain there to this day.</p><p>This ancient pilgrimage site is one of the five panchtirthis of Jaisalmer. The principal miraculous deity of this place remains present even today, and many fortunate devotees have the experience of seeing Dharanendra Dev. Four small temples stand at the four corners of the main temple, housing the images of Adinath, Ajitnath, Sambhavnath, and Chintamani Parshwanath. Since this temple is located at Lodrava, it is also known as &quot;Lodrava Parshwanath.&quot; This Parshwanath is also known as &quot;Chintamani Parshwanath.&quot; This temple was renovated in Vikram Samvat 2034. This image of Lodrava Parshwanath is striking, magnificent, and miraculous, and many miracles continue to occur here. The six-foot form of Dharanendra Dev has been seen here many times, and many pilgrims have had this vision. During the India-Pakistan war, while bombing was taking place throughout the conflict, Dharanendra Dev was seen walking on the spire of the temple, protecting it. Many people witnessed this event.</p><p>Other Temples: There are no other temples, but there is a Dadawadi.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: It is difficult to find elsewhere an image as miraculous and artistic as this image of Lord Sahastraphana Parshwanath. Every pillar, ceiling, and spire presents a vivid display of skilled craftsmanship. This temple, built by Dhirushah, is a beautiful example of masterful artistry. Looking at the images here, everyone feels that the sculptors were engaged in an eager competition to achieve dazzling beauty. The artistry of the main arched gateway is truly beautiful. The carvings of the Samavasaran, Ashtapadgiri, and the kalpavriksha are exquisite and well worth seeing. The ancient wooden chariot, too, is beautifully carved and worth seeing.</p><p>Directions: Jaisalmer railway station is 15 kilometers from here. Bus services and private vehicles are available here. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available, along with an upashraya.</p><p>Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in the &quot;Shatdal Padmayantra&quot; and the &quot;Lodrapuri Chintamani Parshwanath Stavan.&quot; An image of Lodrava Parshwanath is also found at Jiravala Tirth and Bhildiyaji Tirth. There is a temple of Lodrava Parshwanath at Vepery in Chennai.</p><p>Praise (Stuti): You stand guard over this land&#x27;s borders, protecting the entire country; upon your head rest the serpent hoods, and your name is Lodrava, where devotees come ceaselessly, day and night, to meet the Lord with the devotion of the serpent-king. &quot;With devotion I bow to Shri Lodrava Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Lodrava Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Jaisalmer Lodrapur Parshwanath Jain Shwetambar Trust, Post: Lodrapur, Via Jaisalmer, District: Jaisalmer - 345001, Rajasthan. Phone: 02992-50165, Mobile: 9460373142.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-084\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Sankatharan Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-086\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Nakoda Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\"><p>१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन</p><p>Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur</p></div></footer><!--$--><!--/$--><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/40e0f9pufs6g4.js\" id=\"_R_\" async=\"\"></script><script>(self.__next_f=self.__next_f||[]).push([0])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1:\\\"$Sreact.fragment\\\"\\n2:I[20256,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/3vsl20q6wdw12.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0abm728svpw4d.js\\\",\\\"/_next/stati